  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mid November warm and dry spell likely ends \nSunday\n\nRidging at the surface and aloft giving us another beautiful day\nfor late November with highs well into the 60s for most of the\narea. The only clouds in the sky are thin high cirrus pushing in\nfrom the south well ahead of the coastal disturbance. Look for\nthese to make for a nice sunrise Saturday morning, with high\nclouds gradually thickening up through the day on Saturday. High\ntemps Sat will be just a few degrees cooler than today with the\nincreased cloud cover. \n\nThe strong ridging and subsidence over the region has also\nresulted in an Air Quality Alert for fine particles in the Lower\nSusq Valley, valid into tonight. \n\nRain chances increase after sunrise on Sunday in response to the\ncoastal disturbance moving towards the north and east. There is\nstill ample model uncertainty with respect to how far west the \nprecip shield gets on Sunday. For now, deterministic forecast \nQPF ranges from near 0 in the NW zones to 0.25 inch in central \nzones and approaching 0.50 inch in the Lower Susq Valley. Given \nthe large spread in guidance, it is worth noting that about 25 \npct of superensemble members have 1.50\" or more in the Lower \nSusq Valley. On the dry side of the ensemble spread, at least 25\npct of members stay completely dry north and west of Harrisburg.\nLooking at the satellite imagery at 19z Friday, this is weak, \ndisorganized system with areas of spin on both sides of the \nFlorida peninsula. Which of these areas becomes the dominant \nlow pressure system over the next 24 hours is a key source of \nmodel uncertainty that should be ironed out by later Saturday. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cooler and potentially more active pattern into\nmonth-end\n\nWe have high confidence in a noticeable cooling trend for next \nweek. Temps will trend near to below average from Tuesday into \nThursday as the next high pressure system slides by to our\nnorth.\n\nWhile there is still very high model variability next week in\nterms of the precip pattern, there appears to be a general \nconsensus toward a potentially more active setup with 2 coastal\nsystems possible between Thanksgiving and the holiday weekend.",
